  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Digital Defenders Partnership 2024-2027</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">2023
 marked the final year of DDP&#x2019;s four-year strategy and a period of 
consolidation and growth. The programme achieved full regionalisation 
with strong teams in Asia, Sub-Saharan Africa, and Latin America, and 
expanded to Eastern Europe, Caucasus, and Central Asia (EECCA) as well 
as the Sahel and Francophone Africa. The Incident Emergency Fund (IEF) 
experienced unprecedented demand, receiving 635 applications&#x2014;more than 
double 2022&#x2014;with 101 approved grants. The Digital First Aid Kit (DFAK) 
was renewed with improved processes and five new language versions. Sustainable Protection Fund (SPF) support increased to 47 projects, 
while 14 accompaniment processes strengthened long-term protection 
capacities. DDP also supported 15 Community and Network grants, enhanced
 collaboration through CiviCert and the Rapid Response Network, and 
convened six Feminist Helpline conversations to advance digital safety 
for women and gender-diverse defenders.Organisationally,
 DDP completed an internal review that led to clearer management 
structures, the creation of a Grants Facilitator role to strengthen 
transparency and donor compliance, and the development of a new 
2024&#x2013;2027 strategy through an inclusive, participatory process.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Across the world, human rights defenders 
challenge powerful interests and defend marginalised communities. As a 
result of their work, they are often in danger, and with the advent of 
information and communications technologies, these dangers have moved 
online. HRDs are subjected to invasive digital surveillance, office 
raids and theft/confiscation of their devices, online harassment and 
violence, among many other digital threats. These threats are intimately
 related to threats to their physical and psychological integrity and 
often mobilised in conjunction with legal forms of harassment. DDPs work
 revolves around providing&#xa0;a holistic response to digital threats and 
creating resilient and sustainable networks of support to human rights 
defenders.&#xa0;DDP has three areas of work:1) Incident Emergency Support 
which aims to help HRDs at imminent risk or in the immediate aftermath 
of digital threats and attacks. This includes Incident Emergency 
Funding, referrals to our networks, and the development of resources 
such as the Digital First Aid Kit.2) Sustainable Protection Support 
which aims to build HRDs medium-term capacities to manage digital risks.
 This includes Sustainable Protection Funding and long-term 
Accompaniment from our teams of Digital Protection Facilitators in 
Africa, Asia, and Latin America.3) Community and Network Support which 
aims to strengthen the networks of support to HRDs in their regions, 
including through Community and Network Funding, and our engagement with
 the CiviCERT mechanism and other linking and learning initiatives.</narrative>
  </description>
